            'Ramanuja (1017–1137 CE), born at Sriperumbudur near Kanchipuram, was the greatest philosopher of the Sri Vaishnava tradition and one of India\'s three most important Vedantic thinkers. He developed Vishishtadvaita (Qualified Non-Dualism): the doctrine that individual souls and the universe, though real and distinct, are both "modes" (visheshanas) of Brahman (God/Vishnu). Against Shankaracharya\'s absolute non-dualism, he argued that God has personal attributes and is the ultimate cause of both souls and matter. His commentary on the Brahma Sutras and the Vaishnava philosophical tradition he established continue to be central to Sri Vaishnavism today.',
