          id: '102_4_8_1',
          question: 'Analyze the significance of Marco Polo and Ibn Battuta as sources for the history of medieval Tamil Nadu.',
          answer: 'The accounts of Marco Polo (c. 1293 CE) and Ibn Battuta (c. 1330s CE) are invaluable primary sources for the history of medieval Tamil Nadu and South India, providing eyewitness descriptions of the region\'s society, economy, and politics at a time when Indian literary sources are sparse.\n\nMarco Polo visited the Pandya kingdom during the Later Pandya period. His account describes the pearl fisheries off the Coromandel coast and the Gulf of Mannar—noting that the king wore precious gems including a string of rubies—and describes Brahmin traders and the high-quality cloth (muslin) produced in Tamil Nadu. He describes the king as very rich and living in luxury, with a large court. His descriptions of the caste system, the practice of sati (widow immolation), and the kingdom\'s trade with the Arab world provide details not available in Sanskrit or Tamil sources of the period.\n\nIbn Battuta, the Moroccan scholar-traveler, visited the Malabar coast and parts of Tamil Nadu in the 1330s during the period when the Madurai Sultanate was established. His account is particularly valuable for: descriptions of coastal trade and the Muslim merchant community; the political instability following the Khalji invasions; the functioning of Kayal (a major Pandya port) as an international trading center; and descriptions of social customs, food, clothing, and the daily life of different social classes.\n\nThese accounts complement indigenous inscriptional sources by providing outside perspectives on Tamil society, trade networks, and political conditions. They confirm the commercial importance of Tamil Nadu\'s ports and the pearl fisheries, and document the early presence of Muslim trading communities before the political establishment of Muslim rule.',
