          id: '102_4_8_2',
          question: 'Describe the economic life and trade of the Later Pandya period.',
          answer: 'The Later Pandya period (c. 1190–1345 CE) was economically significant as Tamil Nadu\'s southern coast remained a major node in Indian Ocean trade networks, connecting the subcontinent with the Arab world, Southeast Asia, and China.\n\nAgriculture continued to form the economic base. The Kaveri delta and other irrigated regions produced rice as the primary crop. The Pandya heartland around Madurai was known for cotton cultivation and high-quality weaving—Pandya textiles, particularly fine muslin and dyed cotton cloth, were exported to the Middle East and Southeast Asia.\n\nThe pearl fisheries of the Gulf of Mannar and the Coromandel coast were the most distinctive feature of Pandya economic life. Both Marco Polo and Ibn Battuta describe the pearl-diving operations in detail. The king collected a percentage of the pearls as revenue. Pandya pearls were famous throughout the ancient and medieval world—appearing in Roman, Arab, and Chinese trading records.\n\nKayal (present-day Kayalpattinam in Tuticorin district) was the major Pandya port during this period. It served as an international trading hub where Arab, Chinese, and Southeast Asian merchants traded cloth, spices, pearls, and precious stones. Ibn Battuta\'s account describes Kayal as a thriving commercial city with a cosmopolitan population of Muslim, Hindu, and foreign merchants.\n\nThe Arab merchant community had a permanent presence in Pandya ports—the term "Mappilas" refers to Tamil Muslims who were largely descendants of Arab traders who settled in South Indian coastal towns and married locally. This community facilitated trade between Tamil Nadu and the Islamic world.',
