          id: '102_4_8_3',
          question: 'Examine the Tirukkural and its significance in Tamil literature and ethical thought.',
          answer: 'The Tirukkural, composed by Thiruvalluvar (traditionally dated to the early centuries CE, though scholarly dates range from 1st century BCE to 5th century CE), is the crown jewel of Tamil literature and one of the most profound ethical texts in world literature.\n\nStructurally, the Tirukkural consists of 1,330 couplets (kurals) organized into three sections: Aram (virtue/ethics), Porul (material life/governance), and Inbam (love). Each couplet is exactly seven words long—a feat of linguistic compression that makes the Tirukkural the most formally constrained major text in world literature. Yet within this constraint, Thiruvalluvar achieves philosophical depth, poetic beauty, and memorable epigram.\n\nThematically, the Tirukkural offers comprehensive guidance on the good life—personal ethics (honesty, compassion, self-control), governance (the duties of kings, ministers, and envoys), domestic life (marriage, hospitality, friendship), and romantic love. Its ethical standpoint is non-sectarian—neither explicitly Hindu, Jain, nor Buddhist, though influenced by all three—making it universally applicable. The text advocates for ahimsa (non-violence), vegetarianism, truthfulness, and compassion with a philosophical sophistication equal to any ancient ethical system.\n\nThe Tirukkural has been translated into more than 80 languages—more than any other Indian text except the Bhagavad Gita—and has been compared favorably to the Bible, the Quran, and the Dhammapada for its moral depth. In Tamil culture, Thiruvalluvar is revered as "the divine poet" and January 15 (Thai Pongal) is celebrated as Thiruvalluvar Day. The text remains the foundational ethical document of Tamil civilization.',
