          id: '102_4_8_4',
          question: 'Describe the social and religious life of the Later Pandya period.',
          answer: 'The Later Pandya period (c. 1190–1345 CE) was a time of significant religious and social complexity in Tamil Nadu. While the Bhakti tradition of the Nayanmars and Alvars remained culturally dominant, the period also witnessed increased contact with Islamic political and cultural influences, transforming the social fabric of the region.\n\nReligious life remained centered on the great temple complexes. The Meenakshi Amman Temple in Madurai and the Ramanathaswamy Temple at Rameswaram were among the most important Shaiva pilgrimage sites in South India. The Later Pandya kings were great temple builders—they added elaborate gopurams (gateway towers) to existing temple complexes, creating the multi-layered, elaborately sculptured gateway towers that are the most characteristic visual feature of Tamil temple architecture today. Temple festivals and processions remained the central social events of Tamil urban life.\n\nThe Vaishnava tradition, championed by Alvars, continued through the institutions of the Sri Vaishnava community led by Ramanuja\'s successors at Srirangam. The great Ranganathaswamy Temple at Srirangam received continuing royal patronage.\n\nMuslim traders had long been present in Tamil port cities. The Later Pandya period saw the growth of Muslim merchant communities, particularly in coastal cities like Kayal. Mosques were built, and Tamil Muslims developed their own cultural identity blending Tamil and Islamic traditions. The Labbai community of Muslim traders in the pearl fishing regions became economically prominent.\n\nThe Delhi Sultanate\'s invasion under Malik Kafur (1311 CE) disrupted the Later Pandya kingdom and introduced direct political contact with Islamic power, culminating in the establishment of the Madurai Sultanate (1335–1378 CE).',
