          question: 'Describe the art and literature of the Later Pandya period.',
          answer: 'The Later Pandya period (c. 1190–1345 CE), despite political disruptions, was culturally productive—particularly in architecture, Tamil literature, and the continuation of classical arts.\n\nArchitecturally, the Later Pandyas made significant additions to existing temple complexes. The distinctive tall, richly sculptured gopurams (gateway towers) characteristic of Tamil temple architecture were developed and elaborated during this period. The Pandya-period gopurams at the Meenakshi Temple in Madurai, Ramanathaswamy Temple in Rameswaram, and temples in Tirunelveli district display elaborate sculptural programs covering every surface with figures from Hindu mythology. This architectural tradition continued the Chola heritage while developing new stylistic features—taller towers, more crowded sculptural programs, and increasing visual drama.\n\nIn literature, the Later Pandya period saw the composition of important Tamil texts. The Naladiyar (Tamil Jain wisdom poetry) is associated with this period by some scholars. Tamil commentaries on classical Sangam literature were produced. The Ramayana tradition produced the Kambar Ramayanam—Kamban\'s Tamil version of the Ramayana—which, though composed earlier (c. 12th century), was celebrated and commented on during the Pandya period. Kamban\'s Iramavataram is considered one of the greatest works of Tamil literature for its poetry, emotional depth, and Tamil cultural flavor.\n\nMusical traditions remained vibrant. The Carnatic music tradition, which had developed from Bhakti hymns and classical theory, continued to be cultivated in temple contexts. The Bharatanatyam dance tradition was maintained by devadasis attached to temples.\n\nDespite the disruption of the Khalji invasions, the Later Pandya period sustained the remarkable continuity of Tamil cultural tradition.',
