          question: 'Examine the Pandya kingdom\'s relations with foreign powers based on historical sources.',
          answer: 'The Pandya kingdom maintained extensive relations with foreign powers throughout its history, documented by both indigenous inscriptions and foreign literary and traveler accounts.\n\nRomans had contact with the Pandyas from at least the 1st century BCE. Roman literary sources (Pliny, Strabo) mention the Pandya kingdom as a major source of pearls, precious stones, and spices. Roman gold coins have been found in large quantities in Tamil Nadu—evidence of a direct monetary relationship between Rome and Tamil Nadu, with Rome paying in gold for Indian goods. The Sangam text Purananuru describes a Roman ambassador at the Pandya court.\n\nArab traders maintained contact with the Pandya coast continuously from the early centuries CE through the medieval period. The Arab trading presence grew from occasional voyages to permanent settlements. Arab geographic texts (al-Idrisi, Ibn Hawqal) describe Tamil ports in detail. The Arab merchant community eventually produced the Tamil Muslim (Mappilas/Labbais) community.\n\nChinese contact with the Pandyas is documented in Chinese dynastic histories (Hou Hanshu, Tang Shu). Chinese Buddhist pilgrims (Faxian, Xuanzang) visited South India and left accounts of Buddhist establishments there. The Tang dynasty records Pandya diplomatic missions.\n\nMarco Polo visited the Pandya kingdom around 1293 CE, describing pearl fisheries, the king\'s wealth, and local customs in his Travels. Ibn Battuta\'s 1330s account describes the port of Kayal and the Muslim merchant community.\n\nThese foreign sources collectively confirm the Pandya kingdom\'s continuous participation in the Indian Ocean trade network over more than 1,500 years—one of the longest continuous trading histories of any South Asian polity.',
